{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Lake Matano's Iron Curtain: Ferrous Microbes of an Ancient Tropical Lake",
    "date": "2026-10-14",
    "lesson": "Lake Matano in Sulawesi, Indonesia is one of the few modern analogues of the Archean ocean: its deep water is permanently anoxic, rich in dissolved ferrous iron (Fe2+) rather than sulfide, and almost entirely isolated from the mixed surface layer by a sharp, stable chemocline. Photoferrotrophs - anoxygenic phototrophic bacteria related to Chlorobium and purple non-sulfur groups - live in a thin band where just enough red-shifted light penetrates to overlap the rising iron plume, fixing carbon by oxidizing Fe2+ instead of water or sulfide, the same trick believed to have painted the Earth's Precambrian banded iron formations before oxygenic photosynthesis took over. Above them, ordinary heterotrophs graze a thin rain of surface productivity; below, the iron plume is wasted in the dark. The lesson: life finds the one overlapping band where its two currencies - light and electron donor - are both still paying, and nowhere else.",
    "citation": "Crowe et al. 2008, Geobiology; Jones et al. 2011, Limnology & Oceanography - Lake Matano ferruginous chemocline studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Lake Matano, Sulawesi, Indonesia - meromictic ferruginous (iron-rich, sulfide-poor) tropical lake, modern Archean-ocean analogue"
  },
